     7	---
     8	
     9	# Amazon Fantasy Companion — Technical Plan
    10	
    11	## Outcome
    12	
    13	A customer-configurable Fire TV companion, built for the Amazon Developer
    14	Hackathon Fire TV track and the AWS Builder mini challenge (SD-008), that
    15	notices NFL moments relevant to the viewer's fantasy starters, alerts the
    16	viewer while another viewing app plays, helps the viewer reach the game in a
    17	provider app the viewer already uses, and shows a right-side lineup panel
    18	beside unobscured video. It retains experience items 1, 2, 3, 4 and 6 of
    19	issue #1 plus the right-side panel, and excludes every messaging and
    20	community feature. It runs on the owner's 2018 Fire TV stick on Fire OS from
    21	the `AmazonAppDev/hello-world-fire-tv` starter (SD-001, SD-003), reads live
    22	NFL data from ESPN's unofficial public JSON (SD-004), reads lineups from Yahoo
    23	or, from T_yahoo without approval, from labeled manual entry (SD-007), and
    24	runs its backend on an owner-created AWS Free-plan account (SD-005). The panel
    25	route is decided by a spike bounded by spike_panel_max; on failure the panel
    26	runs beside the app's own player with labeled open-licensed footage (Mode A,
    27	SD-006, SD-006a).
    28	
    29	This Lifecycle also delivers (SD-012, SD-016, SD-021):
    30	
    31	- **D1** the product architecture specification, written after approval with
    32	  the D5 shared spec-authoring skill (SD-022, which supersedes SD-013);
    33	- **D2** this program plan to T_deadline with parallel waves and milestone
    34	  definitions;
    35	- **D3** the GitHub milestones, created by the Orchestrator after approval;
    36	- **D4** the shared, repo-agnostic CI/CD capability in the owner's shared
    37	  skills (SD-018..SD-021), landed first, followed by harness hooks;
    38	- **D5** one shared, repo-agnostic spec-authoring skill generalized from the
    39	  GridMarket spec kit (template, build, lint and diagram tooling runnable in
    40	  any repository without a project-specific Python environment), with
    41	  nothing GridMarket-specific (SD-022);
    42	- this repository's Vnv guidance tree (SD-014), its local plus Actions gate,
    43	  and the Scribe developer-feedback deliverables of issue #1.
    44	
    45	The product is a hackathon entry, not a production system: no customer data
    46	beyond the owner's own accounts, no paid services, no hardware performance
    47	claims.
    48	
    49	## Requirements register
    50	
    51	No requirements register exists. The repository is greenfield
    52	(`workspace.md` Observed Systems; `repository-map.md`) and this is not a
    53	specification Lifecycle: D1 is a Markdown architecture specification written
    54	with the D5 skill after approval, not an SDoc register, and no Requirements
    55	System host is bound to this repository. Every requirement below is
    56	Lifecycle-local (`AC-*` / `RISK-*`). Sources are the confirmed decisions
    57	DEC-AFC-001, DEC-AFC-002, DEC-AFC-SD-001..SD-022 except the superseded
    58	SD-013 (written `SD-nnn` below),
    59	DEC-AFC-PROFILE-001, the typed conditionals C1..C14 and the coverage ledger
    60	of `scope-decision-batch.md`, and issue #1 (cited `#1 §<heading>`).
    61	
    62	## Glossary
    63	
    64	### Quantity definitions (copied verbatim from the Scope Definition final batch)
    65	
    66	| Name | Formula / value | Population / denominator | Units | Source |
    67	|---|---|---|---|---|
    68	| T_deadline | Devpost submission close | Single event | 2026-10-23T19:00:00Z | Issue #1 |
    69	| T_yahoo | Manual-entry trigger | Single event | 2026-10-16T19:00:00Z | SD-007 |
    70	| spike_panel_max | Elapsed time from first app install on the stick to the panel-route verdict | One spike | ≤ 24 h | SD-006 |
    71	| min_sdk | Lowest supported Android API level | One APK | API 25 | SD-003 |
    72	| T_judge_access | Last date judges must reach the repo and backend | Single date | 2026-11-20 | Issue #1 |
    73	| aws_free_window | The earlier of 6 months after opening or credits used up | One account | Months | aws.amazon.com/free |
    74	| impl_agent_cap | Concurrent implementation agents per orchestrating session; reviewers excluded | Per session | 5 | Rule 17 |
    75	| gh_private_minutes | Included GitHub Actions minutes per month for a GitHub Free account's private repos; public repos use standard runners free | Per account per month | 2,000 min | GitHub billing docs |
    76	| skill_size_max | Size limit for `SKILL.md`, frontmatter included | Per skill | < 60 lines and < 650 words | `create-lean-skill` |
    77	
    78	### Lifecycle-local design quantities (Planning and Design proposals; approved with the plan)
    79	
    80	These add design constants the confirmed table does not define. None
    81	redefines a quantity above.
    82	
    83	| Name | Formula / value | Population / denominator | Units | Source |
    84	|---|---|---|---|---|
    85	| feed_poll_interval | Period between two backend fetches of one in-progress game's ESPN summary | Per in-progress game | 15 s | Planning and Design proposal |
    86	| feed_fail_limit | Consecutive failed or schema-invalid fetches of one game before that game is marked stale | Per game | 3 fetches | Planning and Design proposal |
    87	| stale_after | Age of a displayed value, measured from its last successful source fetch, at which it is marked stale | Per displayed value | 120 s | Planning and Design proposal |
    88	| alert_poll_wait | Longest time one alert long-poll request waits for an alert before returning empty | Per request | 20 s | Planning and Design proposal; API Gateway HTTP API 30 s limit |
    89	| alert_min_gap | Customer-set minimum time between two released alerts for the same game | Per customer per game | 0–600 s, default 120 s | Planning and Design proposal; #1 item 6 "frequency" |
    90	| spoiler_delay | Customer-set hold between alert creation and release | Per customer | 0–120 s, default 0 s | Planning and Design proposal; #1 item 6 |
    91	| device_code_ttl | Lifetime of one device-link code | Per code | 600 s | Planning and Design proposal |
    92	| red_zone_yards | Distance from the opponent's goal line at or inside which possession counts as a red-zone entry | Per play | 20 yards | NFL convention |
    93	| close_game_margin | Largest score difference in the 4th quarter or overtime that counts as a close game | Per game | 8 points | Planning and Design proposal |
    94	| panel_refresh | Period between two panel data requests while the panel is visible | Per device | 30 s | Planning and Design proposal |
    95	
    96	### Terms
    97	
    98	| Term | Meaning |
    99	|---|---|
   100	| starter | A player in an active (non-bench) roster slot of the selected Yahoo team for the current NFL week, read from the roster's selected position, or a player the customer entered as a starter in a manual lineup. |
   101	| followed player | A starter, a player the customer selected individually, or any player of a favorite NFL team. |
   102	| enabled viewing period | A time inside the customer's monitoring schedule (always on, game-day mode, or custom hours) and outside quiet hours. |
   103	| GameEvent | The backend's normalized record of one ESPN play or game-state change (`design.md` CONTRACT-AFC-EVENT). |
   104	| alert | One customer-specific message derived from one GameEvent, released under the customer's alert policy. |
   105	| launch outcome | One of GAME_OPENED, PROVIDER_DETAILS, APP_HOME, APP_ABSENT, ACCESS_UNKNOWN, PLAYBACK_DENIED (`design.md` CONTRACT-AFC-LAUNCH). |
   106	| Mode B | The panel beside a provider app's own video, by a route the SP-4 spike verifies. |
   107	| Mode A | The panel beside the companion's own player showing CC-BY or CC0 footage labeled "Sample footage — not a live broadcast" (SD-006a). |
   108	| provider app | A third-party viewing app on the Fire TV that can show NFL games. |
   109	| allow-listed package | A provider app package name listed in the provider contract registry (`design.md` CONTRACT-AFC-PROVIDERS). |
   110	| Vnv guidance tree | `docs/vnv/AGENTS.md` plus one `docs/vnv/<area>/AGENTS.md` per area naming Scope, Tools, Review Focus and findings target (SD-014). |
   111	| gate entrypoint | The one script a repository's local hooks, Actions workflow and harness hooks all invoke (`tools/ci/gate.sh` here). |
   112	| shared skills | The owner's skills in the Alphazedehq repository `skills/` directory, linked into each harness (SD-018). |
   113	| spec-authoring skill | The D5 shared skill `skills/spec-authoring/` in Alphazedehq (SD-022). |
   114	| vendored spec tools | The D5 scripts copied into this repository's `tools/spec/` by the skill's vendor step, with `VENDORED.md` naming the source commit (Alphazedehq is private, so public CI cannot reach the skill). |
   115	| replay | Backend serving of recorded ESPN fixtures, labeled "Replay — recorded game data" wherever shown (C2). |
   116	| spike | A time-boxed device or data investigation that ends in a recorded verdict file, not in product code. |
   117	
